  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nRest of Today-Wednesday...A stalled upper level low lingers over\nthe Florida peninsula today. This feature is providing enough\nenhancement to develop scattered to numerous showers and storms\nalong the sea breeze early this afternoon, mainly south of\nOrlando. Expect this activity to continue westward through this\nafternoon, clearing into the western half of the peninsula by\nsunset. Mostly dry conditions then prevail overnight over land\nareas. However, persistent showers and storms over the Atlantic\nwaters may occasionally drift onshore into coastal areas\novernight. The main threats with any storms through this afternoon\nwill be gusty winds to 50 mph, lightning strikes, and locally\nheavy downpours of 1-3\". Low temperatures dip into the lower to\nmid-70s tonight. Patchy fog will be possible over the interior,\nespecially north of Orlando.\n\nAn upper level trough dropping through the central US will push\nthe low NE and merge with it through Wednesday. Deep moisture\n(PWATs 2-2.3\") and the continued unsettled pattern will maintain\nhigher coverage of showers and storms (40-70%) through mid-week. A\nfew strong storms will remain possible. But, 500 mb temperatures\nwarming from around -8 C today to -5 to -6 C by Wednesday will \nlimit the threat overall. Onshore flow becomes increasingly NNE \nthrough the period, but remains around 10 mph or less. Winds\novernight become light and variable, so will need to monitor the\nthreat for patchy fog each night, especially over the interior.\nOtherwise, warm highs continue to reach the upper 80s to lower\n90s, which combine with high humidity to produce heat indices\n98-102 degrees.\n\nThursday-Monday...A strong low pressure system develops into\nThursday morning offshore from the NE US. Meanwhile, the upper\nlevel trough digs into Florida, dragging a cool front southward\nthrough the peninsula. High coverage of showers and storms again\non Thursday (50-70%), mainly along the weakening front. However,\nwill see progressively lower rain chances from north to south into\nthe weekend, as drier air filters into the area. PWATs fall below\n1.5\" this weekend, leading to PoPs 20-40% or less, highest across\nsouthern portions of the forecast area. \n\nA tightening pressure gradient will produce breezy and gusty NNE\nwinds behind the front beginning on Thursday. Winds build to 10-15\nmph Thursday afternoon and 15-20 mph Friday, with gusts 20-30 mph.\nThe highest winds are forecast along the coast, especially north\nof the Cape. Breezy conditions linger Saturday, before winds fall\nto around 10 mph again into early next week. Enhanced onshore flow\nwill lead to poor to hazardous beach conditions this weekend.\nSlightly cooler high temperatures in the mid to upper 80s are\nforecast, though lower humidity will provide relief from heat\nindices above 100. Inland locations could even see lows in the \n60s by the weekend.",
